The Gaming Room was the client I worked with over the span of the course, and their goal was to expand their existing game, Draw It or Lose It, from a single-platform Android application to a web-based, multi-platform system. Their requirements include supporting multiple teams and multiple players, ensuring the ability to have unique game instances, and providing scalability for growth.
One thing I did particularly well in developing the document was analyzing the client’s requirements to the best of my ability and translating them into design specifications. This included identifying constraints and challenges, outlining architectural considerations and concerns, and recommending technologies that aligned with the client’s goals and the most realistic and effective plan. The process of working through the design document was extremely helpful because it provided a structured roadmap, breaking down complex requirements into manageable components that could be turned into action items. It clarified how different parts of the system can or should interact, making it much more clear during coding and ensuring consistency within the client’s expectations.
If I could revise one part of my work, I would improve the cost analysis section by including more detailed comparisons of cloud hosting options and what that means for scalability. This would give the client more insights into long-term costs and help them make more informed decisions. I interpreted the user’s needs by focusing on ease of access, scalability, and performance. For example, I recommended a web-based architecture due to the need for allowing cross-platform access and designed the system to handle multiple game sessions at the same time. Considering user needs is crucial because it ensures the software delivers a positive experience, meets functional requirements, and supports future growth for the game and for the company.
My approach to designing the software involved using object-oriented principles, modular architecture, and UML diagrams to visualize components of the system. In the future, I would continue using these strategies and also incorporate agile methods and prototyping to validate design decisions early and adapt quickly to changes.
